  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Resilient Sustainable Safe Water Supply Ghana</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Safe Water Network (SWN) is a SWE operator in Ghana that operates 95 water stations in rural areas, small towns and peri-urban communities. They serve a geographic area with a population of 420,000 people. The water stations are designed as a micro-utility to move households up the water service ladder and ensure that stations can adapt as population, demand and service level grow through water ATMs and household and institutional connections. These stations are supported by Field Services Capability (FSC) who are responsible for testing, training of operators, monitoring and repairs whose costs are covered partially or fully by the revenues from thestations.Safe Water Network was referred to the Response Facility through Titia/A4A, due to their difficulties during the Free Water Mandate in Ghana. The government of Ghana initiated the mandate in April 2020 and has continued it through March 2021, and since then has only reimbursed SWN for a partial amount of their April 2020 expenses. SWN was concerned both by the loss of revenue and by the public perception of the free water mandate on their business model.As SWN water operators were essential workers, they needed to take actions to provide PPE &amp; provide health messaging for frontline staff and consumers to reduce their risk while water was being distributed. Additionally, due to the loss in revenues &amp; use of reserves, SWN needed additional funding towards operation costs while water revenues could not be collected. They received Response Facility funds for these two activities.</narrative>
  </description>
